AB  - The two common calcium carbonate polymorphs, calcite and aragonite, have been alternatively predominant in both sediments [Sandberg, 1983] and in skeletal carbonates [Wilkinson, 1979], in particular in those secreted by sponges [Cuif and Gautret, 1991a].
   Changes in water chemistry and variations in temperature are often used to explain these oscillating global trends [Railsback and Anderson, 1987]. However, these parameters are not adequate to justify why, under the same diagenetic conditions, the skeletons of sponges and corals do not undergo the same mineralogical transformations, especially in Upper Triassic deposits.
   To interprete this anomaly it is necessary to take into account the peculiar feature of biogenic carbonates produced by the two taxa, the presence of an organic matrix closely linked to the mineral phase. This matrix, secreted during skeletogenesis, has  two presumed functions : binding of calcium ions and orientation of crystal growth. Even after death and burial of the organisms, strong molecular interactions between the organic - mainly proteinaceous - and the inorganic phases will be maintained. The divergent diagenetic trends observed in sponge and coral skeletons could be partially induced by the biochemical differences in the organic matrix. To investigate this idea, the bulk amino acid compositions of organic matrices from four sponge and nine coral (scleractinian + octocorallian) skeletons (tabl. I) were analysed, according to a previously described procedure [Gautret and Marin, 1990]. In addition the matrices of two sponges - one aragonitic and one calcitic - were fractionated by gel-filtration (fig. 1), and the amino acid composition of individual fractions determined (fig. 2 and 3).
   Results, shown in table II, reveal compositional differences between the two taxa. Sponge matrices, which contain low amounts of acidic residues, especially aspartic residues, but notable quantities of proline and hydroxyproline, appear to possess collagenous affinities [Gautret and Marin, 1990]. Amino acid compositions of coral skeletons seem to be more heterogeneous, but in every case, high contents of acidic residues (generally more than 30 %) are recorded. Among sponges, differences related to mineralogy are also detected : the calcitic specimen appears to be more acidic than the three aragonitic samples. Fractionated matrix analysis confirms this tendancy and indicates that aspartic residues are not evenly distributed, but are rather concentrated in the low molecular weight fractions.
   The differences in the amino acid composition between sponge and coral matrices are an expression of the particularity of fundamental biomineralization processes developped by these two taxa. Otherwise they could play a role in diagenesis, since even partly degraded the most acidic matrices, strongly linked to the mineral phase, would inhibit aragonite-to-calcite recrystallisation whereas neutral or basic molecules would catalyse this reaction [Jackson and Bishoff, 1971]. An illustration of such a phenomenon may be found in the Upper Triassic in which aragonitic mineralogy of corals is better preserved than that of sponges [Gautret and Marin, 1993a, b], which from the above investigation would probably had less acidic matrices. In an other way, natural stability of calcitic forms would be increased by high proportions of acidic residues.
   In order to acquire a complete overview of the influence of organic matrices in carbonate skeleton diagenesis, further investigations will be extended to an other important class of organic compounds currently found in fossils, the sugars.
